1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,000
In this tutorial, we're going to cover intercepting HTTP requests and responses in Burp Suite Pro and Community Edition.

2
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Burp Suite can act as a man in the middle between your browser and the website you're testing.

3
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
This lets Burp intercept, view, modify all traffic in both directions.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Burp can even get inside encrypted connections that use TLS or SSL.

5
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,000
For manual testers, this is the foundation of your testing workflow.

6
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
You generally start using your browser, intercepting traffic and analyzing it,

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
and that leads on to other tasks and other Burp tools for more specific actions.

8
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Burp Proxy is the tool to do interception with.

9
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
If we go to Burp, if we click on the Proxy tab, this is the proxy tool.

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
We've got an intercept tab.

11
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:09,000
We've got the HTTP history where you'll see the messages that have been processed.

12
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
We've also got WebSockets history for WebSockets messages.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:19,000
And we've got the Options tab where you can control all the configuration options for how Burp Proxy works.

14
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:27,000
So the easiest way to start is to go to the Intercept tab and click on the Open Browser button.

15
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:34,000
This will launch Burps Embedded Browser pre-configured to work with Burp Suite.

16
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,000
This means that the browser will talk to Burps Proxy listener and it will work with Burps TLS.

17
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
So we click on Open Browser, the browser opens as normal.

18
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,000
We then can visit any website in the normal way.

19
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
And you can see straight away in Burp we have an intercepted request.

20
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:07,000
So in the intercept view, requests are stalled for you to view, edit, forward or drop.

21
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:08,000
So we can read the full request.

22
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,000
We can go in and edit it as we want to.

23
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,000
We can forward it on to the server.

24
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,000
We can drop it so that it just doesn't get processed any further.

25
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:28,000
And we have other actions that we can use to send the request to other Burp tools like Burp Repeater or Burp Intruder to perform vulnerability scans.

26
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Everything else.

27
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,000
For now, I'm going to forward the request on.

28
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,000
And you can see there are other requests that happen.

29
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
And some other ones with page resources and a few other bits and pieces.

30
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
So we click forward to all of these.

31
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Eventually, the page load completes.

32
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
And there we are with the website in the browser.

33
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:58,000
If you get bored of constantly clicking forward, one thing you can do is use this master intercept toggle.

34
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:05,000
So if we just turn interception off, if we go back to our browser, refresh the page, the page now loads.

35
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,000
And we didn't have any messages to intercept.

36
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:17,000
So if you want to switch between testing manually and intercepting and just browsing as normal, this is the easiest, quickest way to do that.

37
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:29,000
One little gotcha that catches out some new users and even catches out a lot of experienced users is that if you leave your master interception on and you forget about it,

38
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:35,000
and then if you start to browse around, what you will see is that the page never loads.

39
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,000
The browser spins indefinitely waiting because its traffic is getting stalled.

40
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:45,000
So always keep an eye on whether you have interception turned on or off.

41
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Now by default, BERT will intercept just requests and certain file extensions in URLs are skipped and not intercepted.

42
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:04,000
This just makes life easier because generally when you're doing testing, you're interested in requests and you're generally less interested in certain file extensions, certain file types.

43
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
But we can configure all of that.

44
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:15,000
So in the proxy tool, if we go to the options tab, we then have options here for intercepting client requests and server responses.

45
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:27,000
What we have here are a bunch of rules that you can define to tell BERT whether to intercept requests based on various kinds of characteristics that requests have,

46
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:30,000
and you can do the same for responses as well.

47
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:35,000
So this gives you really granular control of exactly what gets intercepted.

48
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:38,000
We'll cover all these options in more detail in another tutorial.

49
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,000
Worth noting that there are different options for responses.

50
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:52,000
If you want to intercept responses, you tick this box to say intercept responses, and then you can configure the rules in the normal way.

51
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:01,000
This is another gotcha, or actually a common feature request we get is from new users asking, can we make BERT intercept responses?

52
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
Well, of course it can, and you just need to enable it if you want that.

53
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Now there's one other option, which many people don't know about, but is really useful if you work a lot with BERT proxy.

54
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:24,000
If you go to user options and misc and go down to proxy interception, here you can configure whether proxy interception is enabled at startup.

55
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:33,000
So you can say that you want it always enabled or always disabled, or you can remember the state it was in when BERT was last closed and restore that.

56
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:42,000
So that's intercepting HTTP requests and responses using BERT proxy, and various options you can use to configure how interception works.

